      "id": 11,
      "title": "Archive 11",
      "content": "<h1>Neural Networks and the Learning Machine</h1>\n<p>The architecture of artificial neural networks draws an almost poetic parallel to biological cognition. A dense mesh of interconnected nodes, each one a simple mathematical unit, collectively performs computations that no single node could achieve alone. The backpropagation algorithm—the foundational workhorse of supervised learning—elegantly propagates error gradients backward through the network, nudging each weight toward a more optimal configuration with every training iteration.</p>\n<p>The specific choice of activation function is one of the most critical design decisions in any deep learning architecture. For decades, the sigmoid and hyperbolic tangent functions dominated. But they suffered from a catastrophic problem known as the vanishing gradient—in very deep networks, the gradients would shrink exponentially as they propagated backward, effectively preventing the earliest layers from ever learning anything meaningful.</p>\n<p>The solution that revolutionized modern deep learning was almost laughably simple: just clamp all negative input values to zero. A piecewise linear function. One of the most conceptually minimal transformations imaginable. Yet it solved the vanishing gradient problem almost completely, enabling the explosion of deep learning architectures we see today. Its acronym refers to what it does to those negative values—it makes them unreachable, logically inactive, switched off. The word for that state, in logic and electronics, starts with the letter that looks like a right angle.</p>"
